// Source-reviewed Module 2 Lessons 1–8; bundled locally for offline use.
(function(){window.BE=window.BE||{};window.BE.expressionCourseContent=Object.assign(window.BE.expressionCourseContent||{},{"m2-l1":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> first integrated teaching-content draft for the accepted curriculum&#39;s C01. It reuses the existing speak prototype instead of creating a second copy. The original Basic expressions below are from Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, pp.244–245; the short contexts, explanations, exercises and integration notes are project-created. The compact <strong>show</strong> comparison is from p.252. Clarity, exercise volume and actual learner time still require review.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>When people communicate, information can be expressed, delivered, received, read or sent. The useful question is not “Which Basic word replaces this English verb?” It is:</p><p><strong>What is happening to the information, who is involved, and how does it move?</strong></p><p>This lesson keeps two core strands together:</p><ol><li>expressing or delivering information;</li><li>receiving and processing information.</li></ol><p>A short <strong>show</strong> comparison is included as a cross-topic example, not as a third communication topic. The two core strands are one lesson because they describe the two sides of the same information event.</p><h3>1. Reuse the existing speak prototype</h3><p>The project already has a complete speak teaching prototype:</p><ul><li>BASIC_ENGLISH_EXPRESSION_LESSON_01.md</li><li>the current App implementation in docs/js/expression-system.js</li></ul><p><strong>Reuse that prototype as the opening segment of C01. Do not create another copy of its speak lesson, quiz or writing exercise.</strong></p><p>Reuse its existing three-context explanation, context quiz, target writing task and reverse check. The mapping is:</p><ul><li><strong>speak to a person</strong> and <strong>speak at a meeting</strong> belong to this Communication lesson;</li><li><strong>speak a language</strong> means having knowledge of a language, so it remains a short boundary link to the later Thinking / Knowledge topic, not a communication event.</li></ul><p>The existing prototype&#39;s section 6 and the App&#39;s “next content” preview already list some further source examples (tell a story, give directions, sing, listen and read carefully). This C01 draft develops those examples into the lesson sections below; they are not another numbered lesson. The old preview also mentions <strong>eat</strong>, which belongs with Bodily Action &amp; Contact (Module 2 Lesson 2), not C01.</p><p>The existing prototype&#39;s writing checker is deliberately limited to the target sentence <strong>I say something to him.</strong> It checks that taught pattern only. It must not be presented as an automatic grammar or meaning checker for every expression in this lesson.</p><h3>2. Expressing and delivering information</h3><p>The source examples can be understood through the role of the information and what the speaker does with it.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being expressed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>tell a story</td><td>give it</td><td>The story is the content being given</td></tr><tr><td>give an account / tell what happened</td><td>give an account; give the news</td><td>Information about an event is delivered</td></tr><tr><td>tell someone how to do something</td><td>give directions</td><td>The person receives instructions about what to do</td></tr><tr><td>“as I was telling you”</td><td>“as I was saying”</td><td>The speaker refers back to what was being said</td></tr><tr><td>sing</td><td>give a song</td><td>The song is treated as something given to listeners</td></tr><tr><td>write one&#39;s views</td><td>put one&#39;s views into writing / down on paper</td><td>The speaker&#39;s views are put into written form</td></tr><tr><td>send a letter to a newspaper</td><td>send a letter to the paper</td><td>A written message is sent to its destination, possibly giving a different opinion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A few distinctions matter:</p><ul><li><strong>give it</strong> refers to the story or account already mentioned; it is not a free-standing replacement for every use of tell.</li><li><strong>give directions</strong> is for telling someone how to do something. It is not the same meaning as telling a story.</li><li><strong>put one&#39;s views into writing</strong> describes turning views into written form. It does not mean sending them to someone; sending is a further action with a destination.</li><li>The source&#39;s <strong>send a letter to the paper</strong> remains a direct Basic expression. Do not rewrite it just to avoid using send.</li></ul><h3>3. Receiving and processing information</h3><p>The source distinguishes information reaching a person from the person&#39;s active attention to it.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is happening</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>hear music</td><td>the music comes to the ears of those who hear it</td><td>Sound reaches the listener</td></tr><tr><td>listen to music</td><td>give it their attention</td><td>The listener actively attends to the sound</td></tr><tr><td>hear an account later</td><td>have news of it</td><td>The person learns the information afterwards</td></tr><tr><td>read something in a newspaper</td><td>see it in the paper / be reading it</td><td>The information is encountered in written form</td></tr><tr><td>read something carefully</td><td>go through it with care</td><td>The reader examines the information carefully</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not collapse these differences:</p><ul><li><strong>hear</strong> describes sound reaching the ears;</li><li><strong>listen</strong> adds attention from the person receiving the sound;</li><li><strong>have news of it</strong> describes later knowledge of an event or account;</li><li><strong>see it in the paper</strong> and <strong>go through it with care</strong> do not describe the same depth of reading.</li></ul><h3>4. A short comparison: the different meanings of show</h3><p>The source returns to <strong>show</strong> on p.252. These examples are included as a short comparison because one ordinary word can refer to different events. They are not all communication acts.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>What “show” means in context</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>Topic connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>show the effect of the past on the present</td><td>make it clear</td><td>Making an idea or relation clear; cross-link to knowledge / understanding</td></tr><tr><td>show pictures</td><td>let someone see them / put them on view</td><td>Display</td></tr><tr><td>show old buildings to visitors</td><td>take them round</td><td>Guiding people to see places</td></tr><tr><td>show interest</td><td>give signs of it</td><td>Showing an attitude; cross-link to feeling / attitude</td></tr><tr><td>try to show interest that is not there</td><td>make an attempt to give signs of an interest which is not there</td><td>The attempt pattern; supporting example only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The lesson&#39;s purpose here is to prevent a false rule such as <strong>show = let someone see</strong>. That expression fits pictures, but not every source use of show.</p><p>Keep this comparison compact. The meanings of clear understanding and interest belong more fully with their related topics; they are not expanded into separate C01 sections.</p><h3>5. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet a communication verb, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>What is the event?</strong> Is someone expressing, delivering, receiving, reading, recording or sending information?</li><li><strong>Who is involved?</strong> Who provides the information, and who receives it?</li><li><strong>What is the content?</strong> A story, account, directions, song, view, letter or other information?</li><li><strong>What is the route or medium?</strong> Spoken words, sound, writing, a paper, or a destination?</li><li><strong>Is the ordinary word being used in another sense?</strong> For example, speak a language is about knowledge, not a speaking event.</li></ol><p>Do not choose an Operator or a fixed phrase before identifying the event. The source examples use several different Basic structures; they are not one formula.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Identify the event before choosing the expression</h4><ol><li>Someone tells another person how to do a task. Which source expression fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. give a song</li><li>B. give directions</li><li>C. have news of it</li></ul><ol><li>Someone tells a story. Which source expression fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. give it</li><li>B. give directions</li><li>C. put it on view</li></ul><ol><li>Someone hears music without deliberately attending to it. Which description fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. give it their attention</li><li>B. the music comes to the ears</li><li>C. go through it with care</li></ul><ol><li>Someone listens carefully to music. Which description adds the listener&#39;s active role?</li></ol><ul><li>A. give it their attention</li><li>B. have news of it</li><li>C. send a letter to the paper</li></ul><ol><li>Someone learns about an account later. Which expression fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. put views into writing</li><li>B. have news of it</li><li>C. give a talk</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>B — Directions tell the receiver how to do something.</li><li>A — The source gives tell a story → give it; it refers to the story.</li><li>B — The source describes heard music as coming to the ears.</li><li>A — Listening adds attention from the receiver.</li><li>B — The source gives have news of it for hearing an account later.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Distinguish writing, reading and sending</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches each event.</p><ol><li>A person puts their views into written form.</li><li>A person reads an account carefully.</li><li>A person sends a letter to a newspaper.</li><li>A person sends a letter to the paper to give a different opinion.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>put one&#39;s views into writing / down on paper;</li><li>go through it with care;</li><li>send a letter to the paper;</li><li>send a letter to the paper, giving a different opinion.</li></ol><p>These are different actions: recording information, examining written information, and sending a written message to a destination. The last two prompts share the same source expression; the fourth makes the purpose of the letter explicit.</p></div></details><h4>C. Speak boundary — reuse, do not repeat</h4><p>The existing speak prototype already tests all three speak contexts. Do not repeat its three quiz questions here.</p><p>For C01, retain only this course boundary:</p><ul><li>speaking to a person / speaking at a meeting → communication;</li><li>speaking a language → knowledge of a language, cross-linked to the Thinking / Knowledge topic.</li></ul><p>The reused prototype&#39;s quiz remains the assessment for that opening segment.</p><h4>D. Distinguish the meanings of show</h4><p>Match each context with the source expression.</p><ol><li>A teacher makes the effect of the past on the present understandable.</li><li>A person displays pictures.</li><li>A guide takes visitors to see old buildings.</li><li>A person gives signs of interest.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>make it clear;</li><li>let someone see them / put them on view;</li><li>take them round;</li><li>give signs of it.</li></ol><p>The same English word does not carry the same event in all four cases.</p></div></details><h4>E. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the event described by each Basic expression:</p><ol><li>give a song;</li><li>give directions;</li><li>give it their attention;</li><li>have news of it;</li><li>put one&#39;s views down on paper;</li><li>go through it with care;</li><li>send a letter to the paper.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Sing; the song is treated as something given.</li><li>Tell someone how to do something.</li><li>Listen with attention.</li><li>Learn information about an event or account later.</li><li>Write one&#39;s views.</li><li>Read or examine something carefully.</li><li>Send a written message to the newspaper.</li></ol></div></details><h4>F. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one new context of your own for each group:</p><ul><li>delivering information;</li><li>receiving information;</li><li>writing or sending information.</li></ul><p>Write the intended meaning first, then choose a source-supported Basic expression and explain why it fits. Compare your reasoning with the role/content/receiver/medium questions in Section 5.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. The existing Lesson 01 grammar/meaning checker only supports its registered say something to him target pattern. Other C01 responses require a separately authored and tested finite checker before the App may claim to evaluate them.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>identify the communication event before choosing a Basic expression;</li><li>distinguish expressing/delivering information from receiving or processing it;</li><li>distinguish hearing from listening, and ordinary reading from careful reading;</li><li>express writing and sending as different actions;</li><li>recognize that one word such as show can describe different events;</li><li>keep speak a language as a knowledge boundary example rather than misclassifying it as communication.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 2 — Bodily Action &amp; Contact.</p>","m2-l2":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> first complete teaching-content draft. It is based on Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, pp.245–247. The source examples are kept distinct from project-created contexts and exercises. The lesson has two explicit parts: intake/biting/expulsion, and contact/impact/rubbing. Clarity, exercise volume and actual learner time still require review.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>When an ordinary-English verb describes a physical action, first identify what the body is doing and what happens between the body and the object.</p><p>This lesson has two parts:</p><ol><li>taking food in, biting and putting something out of the mouth;</li><li>touching, accidental contact, hitting and scratching.</li></ol><p>These are related bodily actions, but they are not one grammar rule. The Basic expression changes with the actual action and the body/object relation.</p><h3>1. Taking in, biting and putting out</h3><p>The source begins with eating, then gives more specific examples of biting and spitting.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What the expression makes clear</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>eat</td><td>take food / have a meal</td><td>Food is taken in; the expression may name the action or the meal</td></tr><tr><td>be given more food than one has room for</td><td>be unable to get through it</td><td>The person cannot finish the food</td></tr><tr><td>bite a piece / bite off a bit</td><td>have a bite of it / have a bite at it</td><td>The action is expressed through the Name <strong>a bite</strong></td></tr><tr><td>bite something very hard</td><td>bite it hard / get one&#39;s teeth into it</td><td>The source describes a strong physical bite</td></tr><tr><td>spit food out</td><td>put it out of your mouth</td><td>The outward direction and the body location are made explicit</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A few distinctions matter:</p><ul><li><strong>take food</strong> and <strong>have a meal</strong> describe eating. They do not have to be used as exact substitutes in every sentence; choose the one that fits what is being emphasized.</li><li><strong>have a bite of it / at it</strong> uses the Name <strong>a bite</strong> to express the action. The source gives both forms.</li><li><strong>put it out of your mouth</strong> is more explicit than simply saying spit: it identifies what is moved, the direction, and the place it leaves.</li><li><strong>be unable to get through it</strong> is a supporting example about finishing a meal, not the main expression for eat. It links to the later State and Process lesson.</li></ul><p>The source also uses <strong>get your teeth into it</strong> as a vivid description of biting hard. In this passage it is a literal physical action; do not confuse it with other figurative uses of the same English phrase.</p><h3>2. Contact, impact and rubbing</h3><p>The source then explains several meanings of touch and scratch. The important point is that these actions are not interchangeable.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is happening</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>touch something with a hand, finger or another body part</td><td>put a hand / finger / another part of the body on it</td><td>The body part is placed on the object</td></tr><tr><td>touch something</td><td>give it a touch</td><td>The contact is expressed as a small action or event</td></tr><tr><td>touch something by chance</td><td>come up against it</td><td>Contact happens accidentally</td></tr><tr><td>touch something very hard / hit it</td><td>hit it / give it a blow</td><td>The contact is forceful</td></tr><tr><td>scratch a surface with a pointed object</td><td>make a mark on it</td><td>The scratch leaves a mark</td></tr><tr><td>scratch an insect bite</td><td>give it a rub</td><td>The action is rubbing the place</td></tr><tr><td>scratch deeply with the nails</td><td>get one&#39;s nails into it</td><td>The nails enter the object or skin</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not treat all of these as synonyms:</p><ul><li><strong>put a hand on it</strong> describes where the body part is placed;</li><li><strong>come up against it</strong> describes accidental contact;</li><li><strong>give it a blow</strong> describes forceful impact;</li><li><strong>make a mark on it</strong> describes the result of scratching a surface;</li><li><strong>give it a rub</strong> describes rubbing an insect bite;</li><li><strong>get one&#39;s nails into it</strong> describes a deeper scratch.</li></ul><p>A related cross-link from the feeling topic is <strong>feel a wall to test whether it is dry</strong> → <strong>put one&#39;s hands on it</strong>. Here feel means physical contact, so it belongs with this lesson; emotional or internal feeling belongs in Module 2 Lesson 6.</p><p>The ordinary word <strong>touch</strong> therefore does not always lead to one Basic expression. The context tells us whether the action is simple contact, accidental contact or forceful contact.</p><h3>3. Boundaries with the next physical-action lessons</h3><p>The original passage moves from bodily contact to cutting and breaking, then to movement and body position. Keep these learning tasks separate.</p><h4>Cut and physical damage — next lesson</h4><p>The source briefly says that if a sharp blade is used instead of nails, a person may <strong>cut her</strong> or <strong>give her a cut</strong>. This is a boundary example.</p><p>The source then moves to cutting thread or cake, getting something cut, a thread being broken, a plate being smashed, and a plate that breaks by itself coming to bits. These examples focus on damage, change and result. They belong to <strong>Module 2 Lesson 3 — Physical Effects: Damage, Change &amp; Result</strong>, not the core of this lesson.</p><h4>Throw, catch, hold and body position — later lesson</h4><p>On p.248 the source moves to throwing or sending things, catching and holding them, bringing delicate objects, and the positions of the body. These are about movement, transfer, path, holding and posture. They belong to <strong>Module 2 Lesson 4 — Movement &amp; Position</strong>, not C02.</p><p>The boundary is based on the learner&#39;s decision:</p><ul><li>C02: what bodily action or contact is happening?</li><li>C03: what damage, change or result is caused, or what change happens by itself?</li><li>C04: where does something move, where is it held, or what position is the body in?</li></ul><h3>4. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet a physical-action verb, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>What is the body doing?</strong> Eating, biting, spitting, touching, hitting or scratching?</li><li><strong>What is the body part?</strong> Mouth, teeth, hand, finger or nails?</li><li><strong>What happens to the object?</strong> Is it taken in, put out, touched, hit, marked, rubbed or scratched?</li><li><strong>How does contact happen?</strong> Directly, by chance or with force?</li><li><strong>Is the main point actually the result or movement?</strong> If the sentence is about damage/change, check Lesson 3; if it is about path/transfer/posture, check Lesson 4.</li></ol><p>Do not start by applying one formula to every action. Identify the event first, then choose the Basic expression that describes it.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Identify the bodily action</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the event.</p><ol><li>A person is eating a meal.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a meal</li><li>B. give it a blow</li><li>C. come up against it</li></ul><ol><li>A person bites off a small piece.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put it out of your mouth</li><li>B. have a bite of it</li><li>C. make a mark on it</li></ul><ol><li>A person spits food out.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give it a rub</li><li>B. get one&#39;s nails into it</li><li>C. put it out of your mouth</li></ul><ol><li>A person touches an object by chance.</li></ol><ul><li>A. come up against it</li><li>B. have a bite of it</li><li>C. give it a blow</li></ul><ol><li>A person hits an object very hard.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put a finger on it</li><li>B. give it a blow</li><li>C. go through it with care</li></ul><ol><li>A person scratches a surface with a pointed object.</li></ol><ul><li>A. make a mark on it</li><li>B. give it a rub</li><li>C. have a meal</li></ul><ol><li>A person scratches an insect bite to reduce irritation.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get it cut</li><li>B. give it a rub</li><li>C. send it across the river</li></ul><ol><li>A person scratches deeply with the nails.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get one&#39;s nails into it</li><li>B. have a bite of it</li><li>C. take a seat</li></ul><ol><li>A person feels a wall to test whether it is dry.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put one&#39;s hands on it</li><li>B. give it a blow</li><li>C. put it out of one&#39;s mouth</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source describes eating as taking food or having a meal.</li><li>B — A bite is used as a Name for the action.</li><li>C — The Basic expression states that the food is put out of the mouth.</li><li>A — The source uses come up against for touching something by chance.</li><li>B — A blow expresses forceful contact.</li><li>A — The source connects scratching with a pointed object to making a mark.</li><li>B — The source uses give it a rub for scratching an insect bite.</li><li>A — The source uses get one&#39;s nails into it for a deep scratch.</li><li>A — In this context feel describes physical contact; the source gives put one&#39;s hands on it.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Distinguish kinds of contact</h4><p>Explain the difference between these expressions:</p><ol><li>put a hand on it;</li><li>come up against it;</li><li>give it a blow;</li><li>make a mark on it;</li><li>give it a rub.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A body part is deliberately placed on the object.</li><li>The contact happens by chance.</li><li>The contact is forceful.</li><li>A pointed object scratches the surface and leaves a mark.</li><li>The action is rubbing an insect bite; it is not the same as making a mark on a surface.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. Keep the boundary clear</h4><ol><li>A person cuts another person&#39;s skin with a sharp blade. The source gives <strong>cut her</strong> / <strong>give her a cut</strong>. Why is this only a boundary example here?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is an injury or physical effect. C02 focuses on the contact action; C03 develops damage and result.</p><ol><li>Someone throws a ball across a river. Does this belong to C02?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. It describes movement and path, which belong to C04.</p><ol><li>A plate breaks by itself and comes to bits. Does this belong to C02?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. It describes a change and result, which belong to C03.</p><h4>D. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the physical action described by each Basic expression:</p><ol><li>have a bite of it;</li><li>put it out of your mouth;</li><li>put a hand on it;</li><li>come up against it;</li><li>give it a blow;</li><li>make a mark on it;</li><li>give it a rub;</li><li>get one&#39;s nails into it.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Bite a piece of it.</li><li>Spit it out.</li><li>Touch it with the hand.</li><li>Touch it by chance.</li><li>Hit it forcefully.</li><li>Scratch its surface with something pointed.</li><li>Rub an insect bite.</li><li>Scratch deeply with the nails.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one physical-action situation from your own experience:</p><ul><li>taking food in or putting it out;</li><li>biting something;</li><li>touching something by chance;</li><li>hitting something;</li><li>scratching a surface or an insect bite.</li></ul><p>Describe the event first. Then choose the Basic expression that preserves the actual action and body/object relation. Explain why a nearby expression would change the meaning.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of supported patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary C02 sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish eating, biting and putting something out of the mouth;</li><li>distinguish direct touch, accidental contact and forceful impact;</li><li>distinguish making a mark, rubbing an insect bite and scratching deeply;</li><li>recognize when the topic has changed from bodily action to physical damage/result or movement/position;</li><li>choose a Basic expression from the actual physical event rather than applying one formula to every verb.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 3 — Physical Effects: Damage, Change &amp; Result.</p>","m2-l3":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed first complete teaching-content draft. It is based on Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, pp.246–247 and p.254. The lesson uses the existing Step 17 foundation for past participles, be + past participle and get + past participle; it does not reteach that full system. Project-created contexts and exercises are labelled as teaching material. Clarity, answer coverage and actual learner time still require review.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>A physical event can be described in different ways:</p><ol><li>the action that causes a change;</li><li>the damage or effect produced;</li><li>the state of the thing after the action;</li><li>a change that happens by itself, without an agent being named.</li></ol><p>This is the central decision in this lesson. It is different from C02, which teaches the bodily action or contact itself, and from C04, which teaches movement and position.</p><h3>1. Cutting and breaking: action, result and state</h3><p>The source uses a thread, a cake and a plate to show different ways of describing a physical change.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>cut through a thread or cake</td><td>get it cut</td><td>The object is caused or arranged to be cut</td></tr><tr><td>a thread after it has been cut</td><td>the thread is broken</td><td>The resulting state of the thread</td></tr><tr><td>have a thread broken</td><td>get it broken</td><td>The object is caused or arranged to enter the broken state</td></tr><tr><td>smash a plate</td><td>the plate may be smashed</td><td>A possible caused action/result</td></tr><tr><td>a plate breaks by itself</td><td>it comes to bits</td><td>A change that happens by itself; no agent is introduced</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep the differences clear:</p><ul><li><strong>get it cut</strong> describes having the cutting done to the object;</li><li><strong>the thread is broken</strong> describes the state of the thread, without saying who broke it;</li><li><strong>get it broken</strong> describes getting the object into that state;</li><li><strong>the plate may be smashed</strong> describes a possible result of a caused action;</li><li><strong>it comes to bits</strong> describes the plate breaking by itself.</li></ul><p>The last example is especially important. If the plate breaks by itself, do not invent a person who smashed it. The source uses <strong>come to bits</strong> for this self-occurring change.</p><h3>2. Destroy and hurt are not the same event</h3><p>The source later gives two related but different ideas:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expressions from the source</th><th>What changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a thing is destroyed</td><td>it has been burned / smashed / broken</td><td>The source describes the physical result of destruction</td></tr><tr><td>destroy an animal</td><td>put an end to it</td><td>The animal&#39;s life is ended</td></tr><tr><td>hurt someone</td><td>give them pain; possibly wounds which may do serious damage</td><td>Pain is caused; wounds are a further possible effect</td></tr><tr><td>an attempt to destroy an animal fails</td><td>do no more than hurt it</td><td>The result is injury, not destruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A few distinctions matter:</p><ul><li><strong>destroy</strong> does not have one fixed Basic replacement. For an object, the source describes the result as having been burned, smashed or broken. For animals, it gives <strong>put an end to them</strong>.</li><li><strong>hurt</strong> describes pain or wounds. The source adds that wounds may do serious damage. Hurt is not simply another word for <strong>hit</strong>.</li><li><strong>hit / give a blow</strong> belongs to C02 when the focus is forceful contact. <strong>Hurt / give them pain</strong> belongs here when the focus is injury; <strong>wounds may do serious damage</strong> describes a further possible effect.</li><li>The source says that some attempts at destroying animals do no more than hurt them. That contrast helps show that causing pain or wounds is not the same as destroying.</li></ul><p><strong>Boundary with C08:</strong> destroying an animal describes the action that causes death; C08 covers the animal/person dying. The two Lessons share a subject area but describe different events and different participant roles.</p><h3>3. A short reminder from Step 17</h3><p>The current App&#39;s Step 17, sections 4–6, already teaches:</p><ul><li>past participle forms;</li><li><strong>be + past participle</strong> for a passive or result description;</li><li><strong>get + past participle</strong> for getting something done or bringing it into a result state.</li></ul><p>C03 uses those structures in its own physical examples. It does not teach the whole rule again.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>C03 example</th><th>Use in this lesson</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>be + past participle</td><td>the thread is broken</td><td>The thread is in the resulting state</td></tr><tr><td>get + past participle</td><td>get it cut / get it broken</td><td>The object is caused or arranged to undergo the action</td></tr><tr><td>modal + be + past participle</td><td>the plate may be smashed</td><td>A possible caused result</td></tr><tr><td>ordinary Basic expression</td><td>it comes to bits</td><td>A change that happens by itself</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If you are unsure how the past participle works, return to <strong>Systematic Learning → Step 17 → sections 4–6</strong> before doing the result-form practice.</p><p>Do not confuse a past participle used in a sentence with simply adding <strong>-ed</strong> to make a new word. The source uses <strong>broken</strong>, <strong>smashed</strong> and <strong>cut</strong>; these forms do not all follow the same spelling pattern.</p><h3>4. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you describe a physical effect, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>What action causes the change?</strong> Is something cut, broken, smashed or destroyed?</li><li><strong>What happens to the object?</strong> Is it cut, broken, smashed or brought to an end?</li><li><strong>Are you describing the action or the resulting state?</strong> Compare cutting a thread with saying that the thread is broken.</li><li><strong>Is an agent stated?</strong> If the plate breaks by itself, use the source&#39;s self-occurring expression; do not invent an agent.</li><li><strong>Is the person hurt, or is the object destroyed?</strong> Pain/wounds/damage are not automatically destruction.</li><li><strong>Is the sentence actually about contact or movement?</strong> A blow as contact belongs to C02; throwing or moving something belongs to C04.</li></ol><p>The correct expression depends on the event and result, not only on the ordinary-English verb.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Identify the action, result or state</h4><p>Choose the best description.</p><ol><li>Someone gets a thread cut.</li></ol><ul><li>A. The object is caused or arranged to be cut</li><li>B. The thread breaks by itself</li><li>C. The object is moved across a place</li></ul><ol><li>The thread is broken.</li></ol><ul><li>A. The thread is being moved</li><li>B. The thread is in a resulting state</li><li>C. Someone is touching the thread by chance</li></ul><ol><li>Someone gets the thread broken.</li></ol><ul><li>A. The thread is caused or arranged to enter the broken state</li><li>B. The thread is simply held in the hand</li><li>C. The thread is put out of the mouth</li></ul><ol><li>A plate may be smashed.</li></ol><ul><li>A. A possible caused action/result is described</li><li>B. The plate moves by itself to another place</li><li>C. Someone gives the plate a touch</li></ul><ol><li>A plate breaks by itself and comes to bits.</li></ol><ul><li>A. A person smashes it</li><li>B. The plate changes by itself; no agent is named</li><li>C. Someone gets the plate cut</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Get it cut describes having the cutting done to the object.</li><li>B — Be + past participle describes the resulting state.</li><li>A — Get + past participle describes getting the object into the broken state.</li><li>A — The source uses may be smashed for a possible caused result.</li><li>B — The source uses come to bits when the plate breaks by itself.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Distinguish destroy from hurt</h4><p>Choose the expression that preserves the result.</p><ol><li>An object is destroyed by burning it.</li></ol><ul><li>A. burn it</li><li>B. give it a rub</li><li>C. come up against it</li></ul><ol><li>An animal is destroyed by bringing its life to an end.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put an end to it</li><li>B. put a hand on it</li><li>C. give it a touch</li></ul><ol><li>A person is hurt and feels pain.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give them pain</li><li>B. give them a song</li><li>C. have a meal</li></ul><ol><li>A person is hurt and has wounds. What further effect does the source mention?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The wounds may do serious damage</li><li>B. The person comes to bits</li><li>C. The person is sent across the river</li></ul><ol><li>An attempt to destroy an animal does not destroy it but causes pain and wounds.</li></ol><ul><li>A. it is only hurt</li><li>B. it comes to bits</li><li>C. it is sent across the river</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source gives burning as one concrete way of destroying an object.</li><li>A — Put an end to it expresses bringing it to an end.</li><li>A — The source uses give them pain for hurt.</li><li>A — The source says that wounds may do serious damage.</li><li>A — The source explicitly contrasts an unsuccessful attempt to destroy with the lesser result of hurting.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. Keep the neighbouring lessons separate</h4><ol><li>Someone gives another person a blow. What is the focus: contact or injury?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> If the focus is the physical impact, it belongs to C02. If the focus is the pain or wound caused, it belongs to C03.</p><ol><li>A plate breaks by itself and comes to bits. Does this belong to C02?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The main point is the change and result, so it belongs to C03.</p><ol><li>Someone throws a ball over a wall. Does this belong to C03?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The main point is movement and path, so it belongs to C04.</p><h4>D. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain what each Basic expression says about the physical event:</p><ol><li>get it cut;</li><li>the thread is broken;</li><li>get it broken;</li><li>the plate may be smashed;</li><li>it comes to bits;</li><li>burn it / smash it / break it;</li><li>put an end to it;</li><li>give them pain;</li><li>wounds may do serious damage.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Have the object cut.</li><li>The thread is in the broken state.</li><li>Get the object into the broken state.</li><li>The plate may be smashed by a caused action.</li><li>The plate breaks by itself into pieces.</li><li>Destroy the object through a concrete physical action.</li><li>Bring the animal&#39;s life to an end.</li><li>Cause someone to feel pain.</li><li>The wounds may cause serious harm.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Step 17 prerequisite check</h4><p>Identify the structure in each expression:</p><ol><li>the thread is broken;</li><li>get it broken;</li><li>the plate may be smashed.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>be + past participle;</li><li>get + past participle;</li><li>may + be + past participle.</li></ol><p>If these forms are not clear, review Step 17, sections 4–6. This lesson only applies the existing foundation to physical effects.</p></div></details><h4>F. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>an object is cut or broken by someone;</li><li>a plate breaks by itself;</li><li>something is destroyed;</li><li>a person is hurt but not destroyed.</li></ul><p>Write the actual event first. Then choose a Basic expression that preserves whether the sentence describes the action, the effect, the resulting state or a self-occurring change. Explain why a nearby expression would change the meaning.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C03 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary physical-effect sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish an action from its physical effect and resulting state;</li><li>use the Step 17 be/get + past-participle foundation without confusing it with word-form endings;</li><li>distinguish a caused result from a change that happens by itself;</li><li>distinguish destroying something from hurting someone;</li><li>keep physical contact under C02 and movement/path under C04.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 4 — Movement &amp; Position: Transfer, Posture &amp; Viewpoint.</p>","m2-l4":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed first complete teaching-content draft. It is based on Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, especially pp.248 and 250. The source examples are distinguished from project-created contexts and exercises. Existing Operators and Directives are prerequisites; this lesson applies them and does not reteach the foundation. Clarity, answer coverage and actual learner time still require review.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>When something or someone moves, ask:</p><ul><li>what is moving;</li><li>where it starts and where it goes;</li><li>what path it takes;</li><li>whose viewpoint is being used;</li><li>whether the sentence describes movement or a position.</li></ul><p>This lesson has two core parts:</p><ol><li>moving, sending, catching, holding and carrying objects;</li><li>describing body position and a change of posture.</li></ol><p>The source also uses <strong>run</strong> and <strong>slip</strong> in more than one sense. These are kept as short meaning contrasts, not turned into separate lessons.</p><h3>1. Moving, sending, catching and holding an object</h3><p>The source begins with moving a thing from one place to another.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What the expression makes clear</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>throw a ball to a baby</td><td>send the ball to a baby</td><td>The receiver or destination</td></tr><tr><td>throw a ball over a wall</td><td>send the ball over the wall</td><td>The path over an obstacle</td></tr><tr><td>throw a ball across a river</td><td>send the ball across the river</td><td>The path from one side to the other</td></tr><tr><td>catch a ball</td><td>get it in the hand</td><td>The object arrives in the hand</td></tr><tr><td>hold a ball</td><td>get a grip of it / keep it / have it in the hand</td><td>The object is held or kept</td></tr><tr><td>be given something to hold</td><td>take it</td><td>The person receives and takes the object</td></tr><tr><td>bring or take delicate things such as eggs</td><td>take them / come with them</td><td>The object is carried rather than thrown</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A few distinctions matter:</p><ul><li><strong>send</strong> describes the movement towards a receiver or along a path. The path words <strong>over</strong> and <strong>across</strong> are important; do not remove them.</li><li><strong>get it in the hand</strong> describes the result of catching: the ball is now in the hand.</li><li><strong>get a grip of it</strong>, <strong>keep it</strong> and <strong>have it in the hand</strong> describe holding from related viewpoints. They are not three unrelated actions.</li><li>When something is simply given to you to hold, the source says <strong>take it</strong>. When you already have hold of it, you <strong>have a grip of it</strong>.</li><li>For delicate things such as eggs, the source says it is safer to bring or take them than to throw them; it also uses <strong>come with them</strong>. The choice depends on the movement and reference point in the context. Do not memorize bring = one fixed Basic word or take = one fixed Basic word.</li></ul><p>The foundation lesson on Directives already teaches the general direction words. Here, use them to describe the actual path or destination; do not repeat the whole Directives lesson.</p><h3>2. Body position and change of posture</h3><p>The source next describes the position of a person or thing.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>lie on something</td><td>be on it / be resting on it</td><td>A position or state</td></tr><tr><td>get upright after lying down</td><td>stand / get up</td><td>A change into an upright position</td></tr><tr><td>remain standing</td><td>be on one&#39;s feet</td><td>A continuing position</td></tr><tr><td>a thing stands in a certain position</td><td>stand / be placed in a certain position</td><td>The position of an object</td></tr><tr><td>sit</td><td>take a seat / be seated</td><td>Moving into or being in a sitting position</td></tr><tr><td>make a move</td><td>make a move in some direction</td><td>A change of position or direction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep a position separate from the action that changes it:</p><ul><li><strong>be on it / be resting on it</strong> describes where the person or thing is;</li><li><strong>get up</strong> describes the change from lying down to an upright position;</li><li><strong>be on one&#39;s feet</strong> describes the state after getting up;</li><li><strong>take a seat</strong> describes the action of sitting down;</li><li><strong>be seated</strong> describes the resulting sitting position.</li></ul><p>The same position word can also describe an object: the source says that things which stand are placed in a certain position. Do not assume that every use of stand describes a person getting up.</p><h3>3. Run and slip: short meaning contrasts</h3><p>The source shows that one ordinary word can describe different events.</p><h4>run</h4><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>move quickly because time is short</td><td>go quickly</td><td>Speed of movement</td></tr><tr><td>run for health or exercise</td><td>take a run</td><td>An activity undertaken for health</td></tr><tr><td>run away from danger</td><td>go (off) at a run</td><td>Moving away to escape danger</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not treat <strong>run</strong> as one fixed replacement. First identify whether the sentence describes speed, exercise or escape.</p><h4>slip</h4><p>The source gives two uses:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>lose one&#39;s footing on a little ice</td><td>have a slip / have a fall</td><td>An accidental loss of footing</td></tr><tr><td>a quick, simple action</td><td>take your arm out of your coat; put money into your pocket; go quickly out of the house</td><td>A small movement done quickly and simply</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The second use is a source-listed extension of <strong>slip</strong>. It does not mean that every quick action must be described this way.</p><h3>4. Two supporting examples about balance and movement</h3><p>The source also says:</p><ul><li>a sudden noise may <strong>throw</strong> or <strong>put us off our balance</strong>;</li><li>a blow, given hard enough, will <strong>send us violently against the table</strong>.</li></ul><p>These examples show two different effects:</p><ul><li>losing balance;</li><li>being moved against another object.</li></ul><p>The physical blow itself is a C02 contact/impact example. C04 is concerned with the resulting balance or movement. Keep this as a short comparison rather than a new section of movement rules.</p><h3>5. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet a movement or position verb, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>What is moving?</strong> A ball, another object, a person or a body part?</li><li><strong>Where does it go?</strong> Is there a receiver, destination or path?</li><li><strong>What is the reference point?</strong> Is the movement described from the speaker&#39;s position or from another place?</li><li><strong>Is the sentence about movement or position?</strong> Compare getting up with being on one&#39;s feet.</li><li><strong>Is it a special context for run or slip?</strong> Identify speed, exercise, escape, loss of footing or a quick simple action.</li><li><strong>Is the main event actually contact or damage?</strong> A blow as contact belongs to C02; the injury/effect belongs to C03.</li></ol><p>The same object can be described from different viewpoints, and the same ordinary word can refer to different events. Identify the movement or position before choosing the Basic expression.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Identify the movement and path</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the event.</p><ol><li>A person throws a ball to a baby.</li></ol><ul><li>A. send the ball to the baby</li><li>B. take a seat</li><li>C. have a slip</li></ul><ol><li>A person throws a ball across a river.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get it in the hand</li><li>B. send the ball across the river</li><li>C. be on one&#39;s feet</li></ul><ol><li>Someone catches a ball.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get it in the hand</li><li>B. put it out of the mouth</li><li>C. make a mark on it</li></ul><ol><li>Someone is holding a ball and has a firm hold of it.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a grip of it</li><li>B. go quickly</li><li>C. come to bits</li></ul><ol><li>Someone is given an object to hold.</li></ol><ul><li>A. take it</li><li>B. throw it across the river</li><li>C. have a fall</li></ul><ol><li>Someone carries delicate eggs rather than throwing them.</li></ol><ul><li>A. take them / come with them</li><li>B. give them a blow</li><li>C. get them broken</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source uses send for throwing an object to a receiver.</li><li>B — Across states the path.</li><li>A — Catching is described by getting the object in the hand.</li><li>A — The source uses get a grip of it for having a firm hold.</li><li>A — When given something to hold, the source says take it.</li><li>A — The source says delicate things are safer to take or come with than to throw.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Distinguish position from posture change</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the event.</p><ol><li>A person is lying on a bed.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be on it / be resting on it</li><li>B. get up</li><li>C. go off at a run</li></ul><ol><li>A person moves from lying down to an upright position.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get up</li><li>B. have a slip</li><li>C. put money into a pocket</li></ul><ol><li>A person is still standing after getting up.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be on one&#39;s feet</li><li>B. take a seat</li><li>C. get a grip of it</li></ul><ol><li>A tired person sits down.</li></ol><ul><li>A. take a seat</li><li>B. send it across the river</li><li>C. make a mark on it</li></ul><ol><li>A thing stands in a certain position.</li></ol><ul><li>A. stand / be placed in a certain position</li><li>B. go quickly</li><li>C. give it a rub</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — This describes the position, not a change of posture.</li><li>A — Get up describes the change into an upright position.</li><li>A — Being on one&#39;s feet describes the continuing position.</li><li>A — Take a seat describes the action of sitting down.</li><li>A — The source says that things which stand are placed in a certain position.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. Distinguish the meanings of run and slip</h4><p>Choose the Basic expression.</p><ol><li>Someone runs because time is short.</li></ol><ul><li>A. go quickly</li><li>B. take a run</li><li>C. have a fall</li></ul><ol><li>Someone runs to keep healthy.</li></ol><ul><li>A. go off at a run</li><li>B. take a run</li><li>C. get it in the hand</li></ul><ol><li>Someone runs away from danger.</li></ol><ul><li>A. go (off) at a run</li><li>B. be seated</li><li>C. send it over the wall</li></ul><ol><li>Someone loses their footing on ice.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a slip / have a fall</li><li>B. take a seat</li><li>C. come up against it</li></ul><ol><li>Someone puts money into a pocket as a quick, simple action.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put money into your pocket</li><li>B. take a run</li><li>C. give it a blow</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — This is speed of movement.</li><li>B — This is an activity undertaken for health.</li><li>A — This is escape from danger.</li><li>A — The source describes losing one&#39;s footing as having a slip or fall.</li><li>A — This is one of the source&#39;s quick, simple actions associated with slip.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. Understand balance and movement</h4><ol><li>A sudden noise throws or puts us off our balance. Is the main point the sound, or the person&#39;s change of balance?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The person&#39;s change of balance.</p><ol><li>A blow, given hard enough, sends us violently against the table. Which part belongs to C02, and which part belongs to C04?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The blow/contact belongs to C02; the resulting movement against the table belongs to C04.</p><h4>E. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the movement or position described by each Basic expression:</p><ol><li>send the ball across the river;</li><li>get it in the hand;</li><li>have a grip of it;</li><li>take it;</li><li>come with them;</li><li>be resting on it;</li><li>get up;</li><li>be on one&#39;s feet;</li><li>take a seat;</li><li>go quickly;</li><li>take a run;</li><li>go off at a run;</li><li>have a slip / have a fall;</li><li>take your arm out of your coat;</li><li>put money into your pocket;</li><li>go quickly out of the house.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Throw the ball from one side to the other.</li><li>Catch it so that it is in the hand.</li><li>Hold it firmly.</li><li>Take the object that has been given to you.</li><li>Move along with the object or person.</li><li>Be in a resting position on it.</li><li>Move from lying down into an upright position.</li><li>Remain standing.</li><li>Sit down.</li><li>Run because speed is needed.</li><li>Run as an activity for health.</li><li>Run away from danger.</li><li>Lose one&#39;s footing or fall.</li><li>Move the arm out of the coat.</li><li>Move the money into the pocket.</li><li>Leave the house quickly.</li></ol></div></details><h4>F. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation from your own experience:</p><ul><li>moving an object to someone or along a path;</li><li>catching or holding an object;</li><li>carrying something delicate;</li><li>changing from one body position to another;</li><li>moving quickly, exercising or escaping;</li><li>slipping or making a quick simple movement.</li></ul><p>Describe the event first. Then choose the Basic expression that preserves the path, destination, viewpoint or position. Explain why a nearby expression would change the meaning.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C04 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary movement/position sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>express an object&#39;s movement, receiver, path and destination;</li><li>distinguish catching, holding and taking;</li><li>choose between take / come with according to the movement and reference point in context;</li><li>distinguish a position from a change of posture;</li><li>distinguish the source&#39;s different uses of run and slip;</li><li>recognize when the example is about movement/position rather than contact or physical damage.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 5 — Thinking, Knowledge, Memory &amp; Learning.</p>","m2-l5":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed first complete teaching-content draft. It is based on Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, pp.250 and 252. The source examples are kept distinct from project-created explanations and exercises. This lesson covers mental relations and the acquisition/transfer of knowledge; feeling, physical touch and the different meanings of show remain cross-linked to their own lessons. Clarity, answer coverage and actual learner time still require review.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>Ordinary English often uses one short verb for a mental relation. Basic may make that relation more explicit.</p><p>Before choosing an expression, ask what the person is doing or what relation they have:</p><ol><li>thinking or forming an opinion;</li><li>knowing or understanding something;</li><li>remembering or forgetting;</li><li>learning or teaching.</li></ol><p>These are four parts of one knowledge theme. They are not four separate lessons because the learner performs the same main operation: identify the mental relation first, then choose the Basic construction that expresses it.</p><h3>1. Thinking and opinion</h3><p>The source distinguishes thinking as a mental activity from holding a judgment.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>think about something</td><td>give thought to it</td><td>The person directs thought towards it</td></tr><tr><td>be thinking</td><td>be in thought</td><td>The person is in a state of thought</td></tr><tr><td>think that something is so</td><td>be of the opinion / take the view that</td><td>The person holds a judgment about whether it is so</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not treat these expressions as interchangeable:</p><ul><li><strong>give thought to</strong> describes the activity of thinking about something;</li><li><strong>be in thought</strong> describes being occupied with thought;</li><li><strong>be of the opinion</strong> and <strong>take the view that</strong> express a judgment or position.</li></ul><p>The question is not only whether the person thinks. It is whether the sentence describes mental activity or a stated opinion.</p><h3>2. Knowing and understanding</h3><p>The source uses several constructions because knowing, being aware, understanding and being familiar with something are not exactly the same relation.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>know that something is so</td><td>be certain of it</td><td>The person is sure that it is so</td></tr><tr><td>be aware of something</td><td>be conscious of it</td><td>The person is aware of it; this is not necessarily the same as being certain</td></tr><tr><td>know facts</td><td>have knowledge or experience of them</td><td>The person has knowledge or experience of the facts</td></tr><tr><td>know a person</td><td>be a friend of theirs / have come across them</td><td>The person is familiar with or has encountered them</td></tr><tr><td>understand what one knows</td><td>be clear about it / have a grip of it</td><td>The person has clarity or a grasp of it</td></tr><tr><td>understand horses</td><td>have a good knowledge of them</td><td>The person knows a good deal about them</td></tr><tr><td>understand a friend</td><td>see his point of view</td><td>The person understands the friend&#39;s way of seeing the matter</td></tr><tr><td>understand an argument</td><td>see the point of the argument</td><td>The person understands what the argument is saying</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The source adds that <strong>have come across them</strong> is used more widely for books, pictures and other things one has seen. Keep this as a familiarity/encounter example. It is not the same as being certain of a fact or understanding an argument.</p><p>A useful contrast:</p><ul><li><strong>be certain of it</strong>: you are sure it is true;</li><li><strong>be conscious of it</strong>: you are aware of it;</li><li><strong>have knowledge or experience of it</strong>: you know about it or have encountered it;</li><li><strong>see the point of it</strong>: you understand its meaning or argument.</li></ul><p>Do not flatten all four into a generic meaning of <strong>know</strong>.</p><h3>3. Meaning and memory</h3><p>The source also explains how a word&#39;s meaning and a person&#39;s memory can be expressed.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mean something</td><td>have the sense of it</td><td>The meaning or sense is understood</td></tr><tr><td>remember something</td><td>have a memory of it</td><td>The person has a memory of it</td></tr><tr><td>keep something in mind</td><td>keep it in mind</td><td>The thought or fact remains in the mind</td></tr><tr><td>forget something</td><td>put it out of the mind</td><td>The person removes it from the mind</td></tr><tr><td>have no memory of something</td><td>have no memory of it</td><td>The person does not retain the memory</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Remembering and forgetting form a useful contrast:</p><ul><li><strong>have a memory of it / keep it in mind</strong>: the information remains available;</li><li><strong>put it out of the mind / have no memory of it</strong>: the information is no longer held in memory.</li></ul><p>The source says that when we forget something, it is no longer a part of our knowledge. This is why the two expressions are taught together.</p><h3>4. Learning and teaching</h3><p>The source then describes how knowledge is acquired and given.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is happening</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>learn facts / acquire knowledge of them</td><td>get facts into one&#39;s head / get knowledge of them</td><td>Facts or knowledge are acquired by the learner</td></tr><tr><td>be taught</td><td>be taught</td><td>The learner receives teaching</td></tr><tr><td>teach a subject</td><td>give training or teaching</td><td>The teacher gives instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The roles are different:</p><ul><li>the learner <strong>gets</strong> facts or knowledge;</li><li>the teacher <strong>gives</strong> training or teaching;</li><li>the learner <strong>is taught</strong>.</li></ul><p>This belongs in C05 because the focus is the acquisition or transfer of knowledge. C01 is about communicating information to someone; C05 is about learning, knowing and teaching.</p><h3>5. Keep neighbouring meanings in their own lessons</h3><p>Some examples from the same original source belong to other learning tasks. Keep the boundaries visible:</p><ul><li><strong>feel a wall to see if it is dry</strong> → put one&#39;s hands on it: physical touch, C02;</li><li><strong>feel pain or pleasure / be feeling angry</strong>: internal feeling, C06;</li><li><strong>feel that something is so</strong> → have a feeling / idea that it is so: the source places this with feeling examples, so keep it in C06 rather than automatically replacing it with think;</li><li><strong>show the effect of the past on the present</strong> → make it clear: already shown in C01&#39;s short show comparison, with a knowledge/clarity link here;</li><li><strong>show interest</strong> → give signs of it: attitude/feeling, C06; do not repeat it as a C05 lesson;</li><li><strong>show pictures / show old buildings</strong>: display or guidance, retained in C01&#39;s comparison panel;</li><li><strong>try / make an attempt</strong>: a supporting pattern in the source, not a separate C05 learning task.</li></ul><p>These boundaries prevent the source&#39;s original Complex Acts grouping from being copied as one large undifferentiated lesson.</p><h3>6. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet a mental or knowledge verb, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>Is it an activity or a judgment?</strong> Think about something, or hold an opinion that it is so?</li><li><strong>Is it certainty, awareness or knowledge?</strong> Being certain, being conscious, or having knowledge/experience?</li><li><strong>Is it understanding?</strong> Being clear, having a grip, seeing a person&#39;s point of view or seeing the point of an argument?</li><li><strong>Is it meaning?</strong> What sense does the word or statement have?</li><li><strong>Is it memory?</strong> Keeping something in mind, having a memory, or putting it out of the mind?</li><li><strong>Is it learning or teaching?</strong> Who gets knowledge, who gives training, and who is taught?</li></ol><p>Do not choose a Basic expression from the ordinary verb alone. First identify the mental relation and the role of each person.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Thinking, opinion and certainty</h4><p>Choose the Basic expression that fits the meaning.</p><ol><li>A person directs thought towards a question.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give thought to it</li><li>B. have no memory of it</li><li>C. give training</li></ul><ol><li>A person is occupied with thought.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be in thought</li><li>B. get it in the hand</li><li>C. take a seat</li></ul><ol><li>A person thinks that a statement is true and holds that view.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be of the opinion / take the view that</li><li>B. put it out of the mind</li><li>C. give it a rub</li></ul><ol><li>A person is sure that something is true.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be certain of it</li><li>B. be conscious of it</li><li>C. have come across it</li></ul><ol><li>A person is aware of something but the sentence does not say that they are sure it is true.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be conscious of it</li><li>B. be of the opinion</li><li>C. have a memory of it</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Give thought to describes directing thought towards something.</li><li>A — Be in thought describes the state of being occupied with thought.</li><li>A — Opinion/view expresses a judgment.</li><li>A — Certain expresses being sure that it is so.</li><li>A — Conscious expresses awareness; the source distinguishes it from certainty.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Knowing and understanding</h4><p>Match the situation with the source expression.</p><ol><li>A person knows facts about a subject.</li><li>A person knows another person through friendship or having encountered them.</li><li>A person understands horses.</li><li>A person understands a friend&#39;s position.</li><li>A person understands the point of an argument.</li><li>A person understands what a word means.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>have knowledge or experience of them;</li><li>be a friend of theirs / have come across them;</li><li>have a good knowledge of them;</li><li>see his point of view;</li><li>see the point of the argument;</li><li>have the sense of it.</li></ol><p>The source&#39;s <strong>come across</strong> example is about familiarity or encounter. Do not treat it as a substitute for being certain of a fact.</p></div></details><h4>C. Remembering and forgetting</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the event.</p><ol><li>A person remembers a fact.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a memory of it</li><li>B. put it out of the mind</li><li>C. give training</li></ul><ol><li>A person keeps a fact available in memory.</li></ol><ul><li>A. keep it in mind</li><li>B. get it in the hand</li><li>C. send it across the river</li></ul><ol><li>A person forgets something.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put it out of the mind</li><li>B. be of the opinion</li><li>C. take a run</li></ul><ol><li>A person has no memory of something.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have no memory of it</li><li>B. have a good knowledge of it</li><li>C. give thought to it</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source uses have a memory of it for remembering.</li><li>A — Keep it in mind describes retaining the information.</li><li>A — Put it out of the mind describes forgetting.</li><li>A — Have no memory of it describes the absence of memory.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. Learning and teaching</h4><p>Identify who receives or gives knowledge.</p><ol><li>A learner acquires facts.</li><li>A teacher gives instruction in a subject.</li><li>A learner receives instruction.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>get facts into one&#39;s head / get knowledge of them;</li><li>give training or teaching;</li><li>be taught.</li></ol><p>The learner and teacher have different roles. Do not reverse who gives and who receives.</p></div></details><h4>E. Keep the cross-topic boundaries clear</h4><ol><li>Someone feels a wall to test whether it is dry. Which lesson covers this physical meaning?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C02 — Bodily Action &amp; Contact. It is physical touch, not a mental state.</p><ol><li>Someone feels angry. Which lesson covers this internal experience?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C06 — Feeling, Preference, Desire &amp; Need.</p><ol><li>A teacher shows the effect of the past on the present and makes it clear. Where has this source example already been compared?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C01&#39;s short show comparison. It cross-links to knowledge/understanding here; do not duplicate the whole show section.</p><ol><li>Someone feels that something is so. Should this automatically be treated as “thinks that it is so”?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The source gives “have a feeling / idea that it is so” with the feeling examples. Keep it in C06 and preserve the feeling/idea distinction.</p><h4>F. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the mental relation described by each Basic expression:</p><ol><li>give thought to it;</li><li>be of the opinion;</li><li>be certain of it;</li><li>be conscious of it;</li><li>have knowledge or experience of them;</li><li>have come across them;</li><li>have a grip of it;</li><li>see his point of view;</li><li>see the point of the argument;</li><li>have the sense of it;</li><li>keep it in mind;</li><li>put it out of the mind;</li><li>get facts into one&#39;s head;</li><li>give training or teaching;</li><li>be taught.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Think about it.</li><li>Hold that view or judgment.</li><li>Be sure that it is true.</li><li>Be aware of it.</li><li>Know about or have experience of the facts.</li><li>Have encountered or become familiar with them.</li><li>Understand it clearly.</li><li>Understand how the other person sees the matter.</li><li>Understand the argument&#39;s point.</li><li>Understand its meaning.</li><li>Remember or retain it.</li><li>Forget it.</li><li>Learn or acquire the facts.</li><li>Teach or train someone.</li><li>Receive teaching.</li></ol></div></details><h4>G. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>thinking about a question;</li><li>holding an opinion;</li><li>being certain or conscious of something;</li><li>understanding a person or an argument;</li><li>remembering or forgetting a fact;</li><li>learning facts or teaching a subject.</li></ul><p>Write the intended mental relation first. Then choose a source-supported Basic expression and explain why it fits better than a neighbouring expression.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C05 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary mental/knowledge sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish thinking from holding an opinion;</li><li>distinguish certainty, awareness, knowledge and understanding;</li><li>express familiarity/encounter without confusing it with factual certainty;</li><li>express meaning, remembering and forgetting;</li><li>distinguish acquiring knowledge from giving teaching;</li><li>keep physical touch, feeling and the different meanings of show in their own lesson boundaries.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 6 — Feeling, Preference, Desire &amp; Need.</p>","m2-l6":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed and internally reviewed teaching-content draft. It is based on Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, p.252. The source examples are distinguished from project-created explanations and exercises. Physical touch, knowledge/awareness and the different meanings of show remain cross-linked to their own lessons. All 31 closed practice items have matching answers; actual learner time and user feedback remain unmeasured.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>The source uses several expressions for a person&#39;s inner experience and attitude. Do not treat <strong>feel</strong>, <strong>like</strong>, <strong>approve</strong>, <strong>want</strong> and <strong>need</strong> as interchangeable.</p><p>This lesson has three parts:</p><ol><li>feeling and internal experience;</li><li>liking, friendship, preference and approval;</li><li>desire and need.</li></ol><p>The main task is to identify the kind of relation: an experience, a personal liking, a social relationship, an approval judgment, a desire or a need.</p><h3>1. Feeling and internal experience</h3><p>The source gives three internal uses of <strong>feel</strong>:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>feel pain</td><td>have a feeling of pain</td><td>An experience of pain</td></tr><tr><td>feel pleasure</td><td>have a feeling of pleasure</td><td>An experience of pleasure</td></tr><tr><td>feel that something is so</td><td>have a feeling / idea that it is so</td><td>An impression or idea, not necessarily a firm judgment</td></tr><tr><td>feel angry</td><td>be feeling angry</td><td>An emotional state</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep these uses separate:</p><ul><li><strong>have a feeling of pain / pleasure</strong> names the experience;</li><li><strong>have a feeling / idea that it is so</strong> expresses an impression or idea;</li><li><strong>be feeling angry</strong> describes a person&#39;s emotional state.</li></ul><p>A useful comparison with C05:</p><ul><li><strong>think that something is so</strong> → be of the opinion / take the view that;</li><li><strong>feel that something is so</strong> → have a feeling / idea that it is so.</li></ul><p>The source places the second expression with its feeling examples. Do not automatically replace it with the C05 opinion expression.</p><h3>2. Liking, friendship, preference and approval</h3><p>The source uses different expressions depending on whether the object is a thing, a person, or something one has a taste for. It then adds a more general expression of approval.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>like a thing</td><td>be pleased with it</td><td>A positive personal response</td></tr><tr><td>like a person</td><td>be friends with them / get on well with them</td><td>A friendly relationship</td></tr><tr><td>like good food or pictures</td><td>have a taste for them</td><td>A preference</td></tr><tr><td>like something in a more general sense</td><td>it has our approval</td><td>The thing is approved of</td></tr><tr><td>express approval of something</td><td>give our approval to it</td><td>Approval is actively given</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep the different relations clear:</p><ul><li><strong>be pleased with a thing</strong> describes a positive response to it;</li><li><strong>be friends with a person</strong> expresses a friendship; <strong>get on well with a person</strong> expresses having a good relationship. The source gives both as ways to express liking a person, but they are not identical relationships;</li><li><strong>have a taste for</strong> food or pictures describes preference;</li><li><strong>it has our approval</strong> and <strong>give our approval to it</strong> express the more general approval relation.</li></ul><p>The source gives positive approval expressions here. It does not provide a corresponding negative Basic expression in this passage, so this lesson does not invent one.</p><p>Here <strong>have a taste for</strong> means preference. It is not the literal taste of food discussed in the meaning-expansion lesson.</p><h3>3. Desire and need</h3><p>The source distinguishes wanting something from needing it.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is being described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>want something</td><td>have a desire for it</td><td>The person wants it</td></tr><tr><td>need something for well-being</td><td>be in need of it</td><td>The person requires it</td></tr><tr><td>need more because there is not enough</td><td>be in need of more</td><td>The available amount is insufficient</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A <strong>desire</strong> is something a person wants. A <strong>need</strong> is something required for well-being or because the available amount is not enough.</p><p>Do not automatically replace every use of <strong>want</strong> with <strong>need</strong>. The source gives both expressions because the relation is different.</p><h3>4. Keep neighbouring meanings in their own lessons</h3><p>Some uses of the same words belong elsewhere:</p><ul><li><strong>feel a wall to see if it is dry</strong> → put one&#39;s hands on it: physical touch, C02;</li><li><strong>be conscious of something moving</strong> → be conscious of it: knowledge/awareness, cross-linked to C05;</li><li><strong>show interest</strong> → give signs of it: already included in C01&#39;s show comparison, with an attitude/feeling link here;</li><li><strong>show the effect of the past on the present</strong> → make it clear: C01&#39;s show comparison, with a knowledge/understanding link to C05.</li></ul><p>These examples are not missing from the course. They are kept in their primary lessons so C06 does not repeat C01, C02 or C05.</p><h3>5. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet an inner-state or attitude word, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>Is it an experience?</strong> Pain, pleasure, an impression or anger?</li><li><strong>Is it liking a thing or a person?</strong> Pleased with a thing, or friends with / getting on well with a person?</li><li><strong>Is it preference?</strong> Having a taste for food or pictures?</li><li><strong>Is it approval?</strong> Does the thing have our approval, or are we giving our approval to it?</li><li><strong>Is it desire or need?</strong> Wanting something, or requiring it for well-being / because there is not enough?</li></ol><p>The ordinary verb alone is not enough. Identify the relation before choosing the Basic expression.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Feeling and internal experience</h4><p>Choose the Basic expression that matches the meaning.</p><ol><li>A person feels pain.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a feeling of pain</li><li>B. give approval</li><li>C. get facts into one&#39;s head</li></ul><ol><li>A person feels pleasure.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a taste for it</li><li>B. have a feeling of pleasure</li><li>C. put one&#39;s hands on a wall</li></ul><ol><li>A person feels angry.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be feeling angry</li><li>B. have a taste for it</li><li>C. be in need of more</li></ul><ol><li>A person feels that something is so, but the sentence expresses an impression or idea.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a feeling / idea that it is so</li><li>B. be certain of it</li><li>C. get facts into one&#39;s head</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source expresses pain as having a feeling of pain.</li><li>B — The source expresses pleasure as having a feeling of pleasure.</li><li>A — The source uses be feeling angry.</li><li>A — The source gives a feeling / idea that something is so; it is not automatically the same as certainty.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Liking, friendship and preference</h4><p>Match the situation with the Basic expression.</p><ol><li>A person likes a thing.</li><li>A person likes another person.</li><li>A person likes good food or pictures.</li><li>A thing has the person&#39;s approval.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>be pleased with it;</li><li>be friends with them / get on well with them;</li><li>have a taste for them;</li><li>it has our approval.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. Personal liking and approval</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the relation.</p><ol><li>A person likes a thing and has a positive personal response.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be pleased with it</li><li>B. have a taste for it</li><li>C. be in need of it</li></ul><ol><li>A thing has a person&#39;s approval.</li></ol><ul><li>A. it has our approval</li><li>B. get it in the hand</li><li>C. put it out of the mind</li></ul><ol><li>A person gives approval to a thing.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give our approval to it</li><li>B. be friends with them</li><li>C. have a desire for it</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Be pleased with it describes a positive personal response.</li><li>A — The source says that something liked has our approval.</li><li>A — The source says that we give our approval to the thing.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. Desire and need</h4><p>Choose the expression that preserves the relation.</p><ol><li>A person wants a thing.</li></ol><ul><li>A. have a desire for it</li><li>B. have a taste for it</li><li>C. have no memory of it</li></ul><ol><li>A person requires something for well-being.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be in need of it</li><li>B. be pleased with it</li><li>C. get a grip of it</li></ul><ol><li>There is not enough of something, so more is required.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be in need of more</li><li>B. have a taste for it</li><li>C. give it a touch</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Desire expresses wanting.</li><li>A — Need expresses something required for well-being.</li><li>A — The source gives be in need of more when the amount is insufficient.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Keep the cross-topic boundaries clear</h4><ol><li>Someone feels a wall to test whether it is dry. Which lesson covers this physical meaning?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C02 — Bodily Action &amp; Contact. It is physical touch, not an internal feeling.</p><ol><li>Someone is conscious of something moving. Which lesson covers this awareness expression?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C05 — Thinking, Knowledge, Memory &amp; Learning. It is awareness/knowledge, not the feeling rule in this lesson.</p><ol><li>Someone shows interest by giving signs of it. Where is this source example already compared?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C01&#39;s short show comparison. It links to attitude/feeling here; do not duplicate the show section.</p><ol><li>Someone feels that something is so. Is this the same source expression as thinking that it is so?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. The source gives have a feeling / idea that it is so with the feeling examples; C05 separately gives be of the opinion / take the view that.</p><h4>F. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the relation described by each Basic expression:</p><ol><li>have a feeling of pain;</li><li>have a feeling of pleasure;</li><li>have a feeling / idea that it is so;</li><li>be feeling angry;</li><li>be pleased with it;</li><li>be friends with them;</li><li>get on well with them;</li><li>have a taste for them;</li><li>it has our approval;</li><li>give our approval to it;</li><li>have a desire for it;</li><li>be in need of it;</li><li>be in need of more.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Feel pain.</li><li>Feel pleasure.</li><li>Feel or have an impression that it is so.</li><li>Feel angry.</li><li>Like it / have a positive response to it.</li><li>Have a friendly relationship with them.</li><li>Have a good relationship with them.</li><li>Like or prefer the food or pictures.</li><li>We approve of the thing.</li><li>Express approval of it.</li><li>Want it.</li><li>Need it.</li><li>Need a larger amount because the available amount is insufficient.</li></ol></div></details><h4>G. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>feeling pain, pleasure, anger or an impression;</li><li>liking a thing or having a friendly relationship with a person;</li><li>a thing having our approval or giving approval to it;</li><li>having a taste for food or pictures;</li><li>wanting something or needing more of it.</li></ul><p>State the relation first. Then choose the Basic expression that preserves whether it is an experience, preference, social relationship, approval, desire or need.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C06 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary feeling/preference/need sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>express pain, pleasure, anger and a feeling/idea;</li><li>distinguish liking a thing, friendship with a person and having a taste for something;</li><li>express approval of something;</li><li>distinguish desire from need;</li><li>keep physical touch, knowledge/awareness and show meanings in their own lesson boundaries.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 7 — Exchange, Sale &amp; Commitment.</p>","m2-l7":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed and internally reviewed teaching-content draft. It is based on Ogden&#39;s <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, p.254, with selected examples from the 250 Fixed Word-Groups and the 50 Most Necessary groups. The fixed phrases are supporting contexts, not a second vocabulary lesson. All 35 closed practice items have matching answers; actual learner time and user feedback remain unmeasured.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>When ordinary English describes buying, selling or making a promise, identify:</p><ol><li>what is given;</li><li>what is received;</li><li>who gives and who receives it;</li><li>whether the exchange has happened or is only promised.</li></ol><p>This lesson has three parts:</p><ol><li>exchange and the buyer&#39;s / seller&#39;s viewpoints;</li><li>sale arrangements and conditions;</li><li>promises, agreements and business commitment.</li></ol><p>The central idea is not a one-word replacement for buy or sell. It is describing the exchange from the correct person&#39;s viewpoint.</p><h3>1. Buying and selling: two viewpoints of an exchange</h3><p>The source explains buying and selling as two sides of the same transaction.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>Viewpoint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>buy something</td><td>get it in exchange for money / at a price</td><td>The buyer gets the thing</td></tr><tr><td>buy something</td><td>give money for it</td><td>The buyer gives the money</td></tr><tr><td>sell something</td><td>get money for what one gives</td><td>The seller gets the money and gives the thing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep the roles clear:</p><ul><li><strong>the buyer</strong> gets the thing and gives money for it;</li><li><strong>the seller</strong> gives the thing and gets money for what is given.</li></ul><p>Do not reverse the two viewpoints. If the sentence describes the buyer, ask what the buyer gets and what the buyer gives. If it describes the seller, ask what the seller gives and what the seller gets.</p><h4>Exchange does not always mean money</h4><p>A fixed-group example describes taking a tin tray <strong>in exchange for</strong> a picture. One object is given for another. This is an exchange even though the example is not a purchase made with money.</p><p>So:</p><ul><li><strong>get it in exchange for money</strong> describes a purchase;</li><li><strong>take one thing in exchange for another</strong> describes an exchange of objects.</li></ul><p>The words around the exchange tell you what is given and what is received.</p><h3>2. Sale arrangements and conditions</h3><p>The source gives two ways of describing what a seller does with goods:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source</th><th>What is happening</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>sell goods regularly</td><td>keep them in a store</td><td>Goods are kept ready for sale</td></tr><tr><td>sell what one makes</td><td>put it on the market</td><td>Goods are offered for sale</td></tr><tr><td>rent a house to a friend</td><td>let the house to a friend</td><td>The house is let for someone else&#39;s use</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are different stages or forms of a commercial arrangement:</p><ul><li><strong>keep them in a store</strong> describes holding goods for sale;</li><li><strong>put it on the market</strong> describes offering what has been made;</li><li><strong>let a house to a friend</strong> describes renting the house to that person. Here <strong>let</strong> is the fixed property use, not simply allowing someone to do something.</li></ul><h4>Two different sale conditions</h4><p>The fixed-group examples also use <strong>on approval</strong> and <strong>on credit</strong>.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Fixed expression</th><th>Meaning in the source context</th><th>What it does not mean</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>send a coat on approval</td><td>The coat is sent under an approval arrangement</td><td>It is not the same as saying that a person likes or approves of the coat</td></tr><tr><td>get a coat on credit</td><td>The coat is obtained with payment deferred</td><td>It is not the same as “to one&#39;s credit”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep the difference:</p><ul><li><strong>on approval</strong> concerns the sale arrangement and whether the item is approved;</li><li><strong>on credit</strong> concerns the payment arrangement.</li></ul><p>The source does not turn these into a general lesson on contract law. They are short examples of how a sale may be arranged.</p><p>Do not treat <strong>on credit</strong> and <strong>give one&#39;s word to do so</strong> as the same expression:</p><ul><li><strong>on credit</strong> names the purchase/payment condition;</li><li><strong>give one&#39;s word to do so</strong> names a person&#39;s commitment to give the money later.</li></ul><h3>3. Promises, agreements and business commitment</h3><p>The source gives a clear difference between receiving money now and promising to give it later.</p><p>If we have no money, we may promise to give it later by <strong>giving our word to do so</strong>. Here <strong>promise</strong> names the ordinary-English meaning; the Basic expression is <strong>give one&#39;s word to do so</strong>.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary English meaning</th><th>Basic expression from the source or fixed-group example</th><th>What is described</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>promise to give money later</td><td>give one&#39;s word to do so</td><td>A commitment about a future action</td></tr><tr><td>keep a promise</td><td>keep one&#39;s word</td><td>Carry out the commitment already made</td></tr><tr><td>reach an agreement about a matter</td><td>come to agreement</td><td>The people or groups reach a shared arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>complete a business undertaking</td><td>put the business through</td><td>The undertaking is carried through to completion</td></tr><tr><td>take part in a company&#39;s organization</td><td>take part in the organization of the company</td><td>Participation in a business arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A promise is not the same as payment:</p><ul><li><strong>give one&#39;s word to do so</strong> means that the person commits to giving the money later;</li><li>it does not mean that the money has already been given;</li><li><strong>keep one&#39;s word</strong> concerns carrying out the promise.</li></ul><p>The fixed-group examples also mention a chance of profit when someone takes part in the organization of a company. This is a business context, not a new rule about buying or selling.</p><h3>4. Business context: result and demand</h3><p>A few fixed-group examples give useful business context:</p><ul><li><strong>the profit / loss / interest on a business</strong> describes a financial result or return connected with that business;</li><li>the small slips in the source were <strong>much in request</strong>; this means people were asking for them or wanted them;</li><li><strong>take part in the organization of a company</strong> describes participation in a business.</li></ul><p>These are supporting examples. They do not create separate lessons on accounting, company law or finance.</p><h3>5. Keep similar-looking expressions separate</h3><p>Some fixed expressions contain the same words but describe different meanings.</p><h4>Credit</h4><ul><li><strong>get something on credit</strong>: obtain it with payment deferred;</li><li><strong>to one&#39;s credit</strong>: something is a point in that person&#39;s favour or merit.</li></ul><p>The second is about reputation or merit, not a purchase.</p><h4>Approval</h4><ul><li><strong>send a coat on approval</strong>: a sale arrangement;</li><li><strong>the thing has our approval</strong>: a person&#39;s positive judgment, taught in C06.</li></ul><p>The shared word does not make the two uses the same lesson.</p><h4>Loss</h4><ul><li><strong>the loss on a business</strong>: a financial result;</li><li><strong>be at a loss for something to do</strong>: be uncertain about what action to take.</li></ul><p>The second is a knowledge/uncertainty use, not a financial loss.</p><h4>Finding and discovery</h4><p>The same source passage also gives examples of finding money, seeing that free meals are being given, discovering new land, and losing something. They do not all describe a sale:</p><ul><li>coming across money by chance → C04, encounter/location;</li><li>seeing that meals are being given → C01, perception/information;</li><li>making a discovery of new land → C05, knowledge;</li><li>being unable to put one&#39;s hands on something / having no idea where it is → C04/C05 according to whether the focus is location or knowledge;</li><li>something that was taken has gone for ever → C04, movement/result.</li></ul><p>They are kept in their primary lessons rather than added to C07.</p><h3>6. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet a buying, selling or commitment sentence, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>Who is the person in the sentence?</strong> Buyer, seller, owner, customer or someone making a promise?</li><li><strong>What is given?</strong> The goods, money, another object, or one&#39;s word?</li><li><strong>What is received?</strong> Goods, money, another object, or a future commitment?</li><li><strong>Has the exchange happened?</strong> Or is it only promised or arranged?</li><li><strong>What is the sale condition?</strong> On approval, on credit, or an ordinary exchange?</li><li><strong>Is it really a transaction?</strong> A phrase about reputation, uncertainty, finding or discovery may belong elsewhere.</li></ol><p>Do not start by replacing buy, sell or promise with one fixed verb. Identify the roles and the status of the exchange first.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Identify the buyer&#39;s and seller&#39;s viewpoints</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the situation.</p><ol><li>A buyer gets a thing and gives money for it.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get it in exchange for money / give money for it</li><li>B. get money for what one gives</li><li>C. put it on the market</li></ul><ol><li>A seller gives a thing and receives money for it.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give money for it</li><li>B. get money for what one gives</li><li>C. keep it in mind</li></ul><ol><li>A person gives a picture and receives a tin tray in exchange.</li></ol><ul><li>A. take a tin tray in exchange for the picture</li><li>B. get the tray on credit</li><li>C. keep the tray in a store</li></ul><ol><li>A buyer has no money now and commits to giving it later.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give one&#39;s word to do so</li><li>B. put it on the market</li><li>C. have a taste for it</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The buyer gets the thing and gives money for it.</li><li>B — The seller gets money for what is given.</li><li>A — One object is given in exchange for another.</li><li>A — The source describes the future payment as giving one&#39;s word to do so.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Identify the sale arrangement</h4><p>Choose the expression that preserves the meaning.</p><ol><li>A seller keeps goods ready because they are sold regularly.</li></ol><ul><li>A. keep them in a store</li><li>B. give one&#39;s word</li><li>C. come to agreement</li></ul><ol><li>A maker offers the things made for sale.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put them on the market</li><li>B. get money for what one gives</li><li>C. be in need of more</li></ul><ol><li>A person rents a house to a friend.</li></ol><ul><li>A. let the house to a friend</li><li>B. get the house on credit</li><li>C. take a house in exchange for a picture</li></ul><ol><li>A coat is sent under an approval arrangement.</li></ol><ul><li>A. send it on approval</li><li>B. send it on credit</li><li>C. put it out of the mind</li></ul><ol><li>A person obtains a coat with payment deferred.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get it on credit</li><li>B. send it on approval</li><li>C. keep it in a store</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Goods are kept in a store ready for sale.</li><li>A — What is made is put on the market.</li><li>A — Let a house to someone is the source&#39;s fixed expression for renting it to that person.</li><li>A — On approval is the sale arrangement in the source&#39;s coat example.</li><li>A — On credit is the purchase/payment condition in the source&#39;s coat example.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. Distinguish promise, agreement and completion</h4><p>Choose the expression that matches the situation.</p><ol><li>Governments reach an agreed arrangement about taxes.</li></ol><ul><li>A. come to agreement</li><li>B. put it on the market</li><li>C. get money for what one gives</li></ul><ol><li>A person commits to giving money later.</li></ol><ul><li>A. give one&#39;s word to do so</li><li>B. get it on credit</li><li>C. keep it in a store</li></ul><ol><li>A person carries a business undertaking through to completion.</li></ol><ul><li>A. put the business through</li><li>B. put it on approval</li><li>C. come across it</li></ul><ol><li>A person carries out a promise already made.</li></ol><ul><li>A. keep one&#39;s word</li><li>B. give money for it</li><li>C. let a house</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source says that governments come to agreement about taxes.</li><li>A — Giving one&#39;s word is the commitment to give the money later.</li><li>A — Put the business through describes carrying the undertaking through.</li><li>A — Keeping one&#39;s word means carrying out the promise.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. Understand the business context</h4><p>Explain what each fixed expression describes.</p><ol><li>take part in the organization of a company;</li><li>the profit / loss / interest on a business;</li><li>the small slips made for weighing are much in request.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Participate in the organization of the company.</li><li>A financial result or return connected with the business.</li><li>The slips are wanted or asked for by people.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Keep the boundaries clear</h4><ol><li>A coat is sent on approval. Is this the same as saying that a person has approval of the coat?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. On approval is a sale arrangement; having approval of something is a person&#39;s judgment, covered in C06.</p><ol><li>Someone gets a coat on credit. Does this mean the coat is “to that person&#39;s credit”?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. On credit concerns deferred payment. To one&#39;s credit describes merit or something in a person&#39;s favour.</p><ol><li>Someone is at a loss for something to do. Is this a financial loss?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. It means the person is uncertain about what action to take; this belongs with C05.</p><ol><li>Someone comes across money in the street. Is this a buying or selling event?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. It describes an accidental encounter/location, covered in C04.</p><h4>F. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain what each Basic expression says about the transaction or commitment.</p><ol><li>get it in exchange for money;</li><li>give money for it;</li><li>get money for what one gives;</li><li>keep goods in a store;</li><li>put what one makes on the market;</li><li>let a house to a friend;</li><li>send a coat on approval;</li><li>get a coat on credit;</li><li>give one&#39;s word to do so;</li><li>keep one&#39;s word;</li><li>come to agreement;</li><li>put the business through;</li><li>take one object in exchange for another;</li><li>take part in the organization of a company;</li><li>small slips much in request.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>The buyer receives the thing and gives money for it.</li><li>The buyer gives money to receive the thing.</li><li>The seller receives money for the thing given.</li><li>Goods are kept ready for sale.</li><li>Goods made by the seller are offered for sale.</li><li>The house is rented to the friend.</li><li>The coat is sent under an approval arrangement.</li><li>The coat is obtained with payment deferred.</li><li>The person commits to doing something later.</li><li>The person carries out the promise already made.</li><li>The people reach an agreed arrangement.</li><li>The business undertaking is carried through to completion.</li><li>One object is given and another is received.</li><li>The person participates in organizing the company.</li><li>The small slips are wanted or requested by people.</li></ol></div></details><h4>G. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>buying something and giving money for it;</li><li>selling something and receiving money;</li><li>offering goods for sale;</li><li>renting a house to someone;</li><li>obtaining something on credit or receiving it on approval;</li><li>making a promise to give money later;</li><li>carrying out a business undertaking.</li></ul><p>First identify who gives what and who gets what. Then choose the Basic expression that preserves whether the transaction has happened, is arranged, or is only promised.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C07 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary transaction sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>describe buying from the buyer&#39;s viewpoint and selling from the seller&#39;s viewpoint;</li><li>distinguish an exchange of objects from a purchase for money;</li><li>distinguish keeping goods in a store from putting goods on the market;</li><li>distinguish on approval from on credit;</li><li>distinguish a promise from payment and keeping one&#39;s word from giving one&#39;s word;</li><li>recognize the selected business fixed phrases without confusing them with unrelated meanings such as “to one&#39;s credit” or “at a loss”.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 8 — State and Process: Change, Entry &amp; Ending.</p>","m2-l8":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed and internally reviewed teaching-content draft. It is based on <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, printed pp.255–256, and selected Fixed Word-Groups. The source&#39;s use of the ordinary-English word <strong>finish</strong> is explicitly not treated as a Basic expression. All 34 closed practice prompts have matching answers. Actual learner time and user feedback remain unmeasured; the lesson is not user-tested or App-integrated.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>This lesson is not a list of verbs with one replacement each. The learner identifies what is happening:</p><ol><li>a Quality is changing;</li><li>a process is under way;</li><li>something is entering a state;</li><li>something is already in a state;</li><li>an event takes place;</li><li>an activity or process comes to an end.</li></ol><p>The same ordinary-English word can describe different relations. Choose the Basic expression only after identifying the relation.</p><h3>1. A change in Quality or a process under way</h3><p>The source gives four examples of a change in Quality:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary-English meaning</th><th>Basic expression in the source</th><th>What it describes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>boys and girls grow taller</td><td>get taller</td><td>A change in height</td></tr><tr><td>trees grow higher</td><td>get higher</td><td>A change in height</td></tr><tr><td>old men grow fatter</td><td>get fatter</td><td>A change in size</td></tr><tr><td>a person&#39;s powers grow greater</td><td>get greater</td><td>An increase in degree or strength</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The source then gives a different expression for things that are increasing in these ways:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary-English meaning</th><th>Basic expression in the source</th><th>What it describes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>be growing</td><td>be in the process of growth or development</td><td>The process is under way</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep the two constructions distinct:</p><ul><li><strong>get + Quality</strong> describes a change into a Quality: get taller, get higher, get fatter, get greater;</li><li><strong>be in the process of + Name</strong> describes the process that is under way: be in the process of growth or development.</li></ul><p>These are the source&#39;s examples. Do not turn them into a rule that every use of grow must be translated by get, or that every ongoing action must use be in the process of.</p><h3>2. Entering a state or being in that state</h3><p>The source explains sleep with two expressions:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ordinary-English meaning</th><th>Basic expression in the source</th><th>What it describes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>go to sleep</td><td>go to sleep</td><td>Entering the state of sleep</td></tr><tr><td>be asleep / be in sleep</td><td>be in sleep</td><td>Being in the state of sleep</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The decision is:</p><ul><li><strong>go to sleep</strong> — the change into the state;</li><li><strong>be in sleep</strong> — the state itself.</li></ul><p>Keep the exact source expression <strong>be in sleep</strong> when studying this Basic example. It is not a reason to replace every sentence containing sleep with the same phrase.</p><h3>3. Expressing the end of a life</h3><p>The source gives three expressions for die:</p><ul><li>go to one&#39;s death;</li><li>come to one&#39;s end;</li><li>take one&#39;s last breath.</li></ul><p>All three describe the end of a life. They are alternative source expressions, not three unrelated grammar rules.</p><p>Keep the perspective distinct from C03: C03 describes an action performed on an animal to cause its death; C08 describes the animal/person dying, not the agent&#39;s action.</p><p>Do not replace the exact phrase <strong>come to one&#39;s end</strong> with <strong>come to an end</strong> and then assume the source has supplied a general translation for every kind of ending. The example on this page is about death.</p><h3>4. Beginning, and the source boundary around finish</h3><p>The source gives:</p><ul><li>begin → make a start.</li></ul><p>There is an important boundary here. The source also says, in its explanatory English, that everything that begins will later finish. It then uses <strong>make a start</strong> to describe beginning the physical acts.</p><p>That sentence does <strong>not</strong> give a Basic equivalent for the general verb <strong>finish</strong>. The word finish is not one of the 850 base words. Therefore:</p><ul><li><strong>make a start</strong> is the source-provided Basic expression for begin;</li><li><strong>finish → finish</strong> is not a valid Basic mapping from this passage;</li><li>this lesson does not invent a universal Basic replacement for finish.</li></ul><p>The lesson still teaches ending and cessation through the source&#39;s death expressions and the fixed groups below. It does not pretend that the source has provided a general finish formula.</p><h3>5. Fixed groups that reinforce state, change and cessation</h3><p>The 50 Most Necessary Fixed Word-Groups contain several expressions that reinforce this lesson:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Fixed group</th><th>Relation</th><th>What the learner should notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>in motion</td><td>State / activity</td><td>Something is moving</td></tr><tr><td>in use (operation)</td><td>State / activity</td><td>Something is being used or is operating</td></tr><tr><td>go to sleep</td><td>Entry into a state</td><td>A person enters the state of sleep</td></tr><tr><td>lights go out</td><td>Change of state</td><td>The lights stop shining</td></tr><tr><td>come to a stop</td><td>Reaching a stopped state</td><td>The subject reaches a stopping point</td></tr><tr><td>put a stop to an activity</td><td>Causing cessation</td><td>Someone or something causes the activity to stop</td></tr><tr><td>take place</td><td>Event occurrence</td><td>An event happens</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two expressions about stopping must not be collapsed:</p><ul><li><strong>come to a stop</strong> describes the subject reaching a stopped state;</li><li><strong>put a stop to</strong> describes causing an activity or process to stop.</li></ul><p><strong>In the end</strong> is primarily a C11 Time &amp; Sequence item. It may be cross-linked here when the context is about the eventual outcome of a process, but it is not a core C08 state/process expression.</p><p>These fixed groups are selected reinforcement. They do not create a second vocabulary lesson.</p><h3>6. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When the ordinary-English sentence concerns growth, sleep, death, beginning or stopping, ask:</p><ol><li>Is a Quality changing, or is a process under way?</li><li>Is something entering a state, or is it already in that state?</li><li>Is the sentence about the end of a life?</li><li>Is it about beginning? If so, use the source&#39;s <strong>make a start</strong> expression.</li><li>Is an event taking place, or is an activity being brought to a stop?</li><li>Is the phrase <strong>in the end</strong> being used for time/sequence? If so, keep its main teaching home in C11.</li></ol><p>Do not choose a phrase just because it contains a similar word. Preserve the relation in the sentence.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Change, process, entry and state</h4><p>Choose the expression that preserves the meaning.</p><ol><li>A boy grows taller.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get taller</li><li>B. be in the process of growth</li><li>C. go to sleep</li></ul><ol><li>A tree is in the middle of growing.</li></ol><ul><li>A. get higher</li><li>B. be in the process of growth</li><li>C. come to a stop</li></ul><ol><li>A child is entering the state of sleep.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be in sleep</li><li>B. go to sleep</li><li>C. take place</li></ul><ol><li>A person is already in the state of sleep.</li></ol><ul><li>A. go to sleep</li><li>B. be in sleep</li><li>C. make a start</li></ul><ol><li>A person dies. Which expressions does the source give?</li></ol><ul><li>A. go to one&#39;s death / come to one&#39;s end / take one&#39;s last breath</li><li>B. get taller / get higher</li><li>C. in motion / in use</li></ul><ol><li>A person begins a task.</li></ol><ul><li>A. make a start</li><li>B. put a stop to it</li><li>C. be in sleep</li></ul><ol><li>A person finishes a task. Does this passage give a Basic equivalent for the general meaning of finish?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes: finish → finish</li><li>B. No: finish occurs in explanatory English, but no Basic equivalent is supplied here</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source uses get + Quality for the change in height.</li><li>B — The source uses be in the process of growth or development for the process under way.</li><li>B — Go to sleep describes entering the state.</li><li>B — Be in sleep describes being in the state.</li><li>A — These are the three expressions the source gives for die.</li><li>A — The source gives make a start for begin.</li><li>B — The sentence with finish is explanatory English. It is not a Basic translation example, and finish is not in the 850 base list.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. State, event and stopping fixed groups</h4><p>Match the situation with the fixed group.</p><ol><li>A machine is operating.</li><li>A thing is moving.</li><li>The lights stop shining.</li><li>A moving thing reaches a stopped state.</li><li>Someone causes an activity to stop.</li><li>An event happens.</li><li>A story uses in the end to show its final temporal outcome.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>in use (operation);</li><li>in motion;</li><li>lights go out;</li><li>come to a stop;</li><li>put a stop to the activity;</li><li>take place;</li><li>in the end — its primary lesson is C11 Time &amp; Sequence; cross-link to C08 only when discussing the eventual outcome of a process.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. Keep neighbouring lessons separate</h4><ol><li>A plate breaks by itself and comes to bits. Which lesson teaches the physical change/result?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C03 — Physical Effects: Damage, Change &amp; Result. C08 is not the lesson for physical breakage.</p><ol><li>A ball is sent over a wall. Which lesson teaches path and spatial movement?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C04 — Movement &amp; Position. C08 does not teach spatial path or destination.</p><ol><li>Someone asks, “Shall I do it?” Which lesson teaches the source&#39;s intention/advisability distinction?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C09 — Ability, Obligation &amp; Intention. C08 uses make a start for beginning; it does not teach shall.</p><h4>D. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the relation described by each Basic expression.</p><ol><li>get taller;</li><li>get higher;</li><li>get fatter;</li><li>get greater;</li><li>be in the process of growth or development;</li><li>go to sleep;</li><li>be in sleep;</li><li>go to one&#39;s death;</li><li>come to one&#39;s end;</li><li>take one&#39;s last breath;</li><li>make a start;</li><li>in motion;</li><li>in use;</li><li>lights go out;</li><li>come to a stop;</li><li>put a stop to an activity;</li><li>take place.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Become taller; the height changes.</li><li>Become higher; the height or level changes.</li><li>Become fatter; the size changes.</li><li>Become greater; the degree or strength increases.</li><li>The process of growth or development is under way.</li><li>Enter the state of sleep.</li><li>Be in the state of sleep.</li><li>Die.</li><li>Die; reach the end of one&#39;s life.</li><li>Die; take the final breath.</li><li>Begin.</li><li>Be moving.</li><li>Be operating or being used.</li><li>The lights stop shining.</li><li>Reach a stopped state.</li><li>Cause the activity to stop.</li><li>Happen; an event occurs.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>a change in height, size or degree;</li><li>a process of growth that is under way;</li><li>entering sleep or being in sleep;</li><li>expressing death with one of the source&#39;s expressions;</li><li>beginning a task;</li><li>an object or activity coming to a stop;</li><li>causing an activity to stop;</li><li>an event taking place.</li></ul><p>First identify the relation. Then choose the source expression that preserves it.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C08 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary state/process sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish a change in Quality from a process under way;</li><li>distinguish entering a state from being in that state;</li><li>recognize the source&#39;s three expressions for death;</li><li>use make a start for begin;</li><li>recognize that this passage does not provide a Basic equivalent for the general verb finish;</li><li>distinguish a state, an event taking place, reaching a stop and causing something to stop;</li><li>keep physical change, spatial movement, time sequence and modal intention in their own lessons.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 9 — Ability, Obligation &amp; Intention.</p>"});})();
